      ],
      "run": {
        "answer": "For rusty steel outdoors, the verified selection chart recommends flux-core (FCAW) and Stick (SMAW) equally, both scoring 8. Both are ideal for outdoor or windy conditions and forgiving on rusty or dirty steels. MIG and TIG did not appear in the recommendations for this scenario.\n\nSources: files/selection-chart.pdf p.1\n\nFlux-core requires low skill, needs no shielding gas, and handles material from 18 gauge to 5/16 inch thick. Stick requires moderate skill, also needs no shielding gas, and handles material from 10 gauge to 1/2 inch thick, making it better for thicker materials.\n\nSources: files/selection-chart.pdf p.1\n\nBoth processes share wind-resistance advantages and produce more spatter. Flux-core offers high deposition rates and good out-of-position welding; Stick offers deep penetration. Your choice depends on material thickness and skill level—flux-core if you want lower skill requirement, Stick if your work is thicker than 5/16 inch.\n\nSources: files/selection-chart.pdf p.1",
        "tools": [
          "recommend_process"
        ],
