      "run": {
        "answer": "Do not proceed with this repair without a qualified welding professional. A failed weld on an automotive vehicle can cause severe downstream harm.\n\nSources: Safety-critical repair classification [inference]\n\nBefore work commences, a qualified professional must resolve these critical safety questions: Is the workpiece sealed, pressurized, or previously used for fuel or chemicals? Is the work area completely dry? Is ventilation adequate, and is this a confined space? Have nearby combustibles been removed or protected? Do you have the required welding PPE? Has the base material and every coating been positively identified?\n\nSources: Safety-critical repair classification [inference]\n\nUnder professional guidance, the MIG/GMAW process is recommended for aluminum automotive body repair. MIG produces clean welds and is suitable for material thicknesses from 22 gauge to 3/8 inch.\n\nSources: files/selection-chart.pdf p.1\n\nA spool gun is required equipment for MIG welding of aluminum.\n\nSources: files/selection-chart.pdf p.1",
